# Optional settings for docker compose. The report needs NO secrets — it reads
# only public RPC data — so this file is convenience, not a requirement.
#
# Usage on the server:
#   1. cp .env.example .env
#   2. adjust values
#   3. docker compose up -d   (Compose reads .env automatically)

# Qubic RPC endpoint the report is built from. Point this at your own node or a
# mirror if you don't want to depend on the public endpoint.
QUBIC_RPC_BASE=https://rpc.qubic.org

# Self-reporting registry. Leave unset to use the pools.json baked into the image;
# set it to run your own (mount it via the volume in docker-compose.yaml).
#QDR_REGISTRY=/data/pools.json

# Persistent store (CONCEPT §5.1). Defaults to $DATA_DIR/qdr.db, i.e. inside the
# volume — closed epochs are sealed there and survive restarts and RPC outages.
#QDR_DB=/data/qdr.db

# How stale the running epoch may get (seconds) before a request recomputes it.
# Closed epochs are never recomputed, so this only affects the live epoch.
#QDR_LIVE_MAX_AGE=300

# Arbitrator identity used to derive per-computor revenue. Still unverified in the
# bundled default — run `python scripts/ingest.py --verify-arbitrator` and set the
# confirmed identity here (see docs/DATA_SOURCES.md §4).
#QUBIC_ARBITRATOR=

# Bob node — the ONLY public source that carries the protocol's epoch-end computor
# payouts (docs/DATA_SOURCES.md §7). Defaults to the public instance; point this at
# your own node (RPC port 40420) so the report does not depend on someone else's.
#QDR_BOB_URL=http://10.0.0.5:40420

# Live pulse cache (seconds). The tick moves ~1.6/s, so a few seconds of caching
# costs nothing and protects the upstream from many dashboards polling at once.
#QDR_PULSE_TTL=10

# How often the ingest worker samples live mining state into the store. Mining
# state is not on the RPC and is not replayable, so a reading not taken is lost
# for good; this is what gives the mining page a curve rather than one number.
# Matches QDR_MINING_TTL by default, so no reading the API already fetched is
# thrown away. One epoch of samples is ~0.8 MB and only the last two epochs are
# kept, so the cost stays around 2 MB however long this runs.
#QDR_MINING_SAMPLE_INTERVAL=30

# Smart-contract registry: where contract index -> name comes from. This is the
# same file the official Qubic explorer renders, so a newly deployed contract
# shows up by name on its own. Cached 24 h; an unreachable registry costs names,
# never correctness (the page falls back to the bare index).
#QDR_CONTRACTS_URL=https://static.qubic.org/v1/general/data/smart_contracts.json
#QDR_CONTRACTS_TTL=86400

# ── Market price ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read from Qubic's own /v1/latest-stats, the endpoint this project already uses
# for supply and tick quality. Exchange APIs are deliberately NOT used: MEXC's
# terms (clause 17d) prohibit "data feeding or streaming services that make use
# of any market data of MEXC" without written consent, and storing their bars to
# republish them here would be exactly that. Qubic's stats service holds the
# licence for the price it publishes, so this stays inside one set of terms.
#
# The cost, stated plainly: no trading volume. The RPC publishes none.
#
# How often the price is polled. One minute matches the upstream refresh
# (qubic-stats-service scrapes every 60s), so polling faster only re-reads the
# same figure. A row is stored when the price CHANGES; a poll that finds it
# unchanged extends the current row and increments its poll count, so a flat
# market costs almost no rows and a gap is still distinguishable from calm.
#QDR_PRICE_SAMPLE_INTERVAL=60

# How long the API may serve a cached price answer. Kept well under the sampling
# interval so a cached response is never older than the reading behind it.
#QDR_PRICE_TTL=15
#QDR_PRICE_SERIES_TTL=20
